The adequacy/pinning review finds the claim’s `Module(...)` constructor is mechanically identical to regenerated `solution.mpy` after only normalizing K’s explicit empty-list spelling. The arbitrary continuation is preserved, and a body mutation of the executed constructor fails on the expected `Int2String(B) = Int2String(X)` obligation, so the theorem is body-sensitive. The fatal gap remains contract adequacy: a satisfying state `X=0, B=2, CONT=.K` proves `"0"` for the submitted body, while trusted canonical execution returns `""`; negative integers are also excluded without an explicit prompt restriction.
The claim pins the exact translated body and is body-sensitive, but its proven behavior disagrees with the trusted canonical at a satisfying zero input and excludes prompt-unrestricted negative integers.
